from pathlib import Path
exec(Path('verification/state-guides/research/build-b.py').read_text(encoding='utf-8-sig').split("add('Maine'")[0])
add('Louisiana','LA','Louisiana bookkeeping for state and parish sales records, digital product purchases, payroll accounts and location reconciliations.','Louisiana books need transaction locations and separate state and parish tax support, including current digital product records.',[
('State And Parish Sales Support',[1,2],['Louisiana state and parish taxes involve separate taxing jurisdictions. Maintain the location behind each sale and preserve state and local return acknowledgments. A centralized filing portal does not remove the need to explain which transactions belong to each jurisdiction.','The current dealer record statute includes tangible property, digital products and taxable services. Keep the product description, transaction date, delivery information and evidence supporting any deduction. Review purchased software and digital products with the preparer using current rules rather than relying on an old tax code in the accounting system.']),
('Payroll Accounts And Withholding',[3],['Louisiana requires withholding returns and payments electronically. Its current employer page requires a quarterly return for each assigned period, including zero periods, and an annual reconciliation. An active account does not close merely because the business stops paying wages.','As a payroll control, preserve worker certificates, wage registers, actual work locations and confirmations from LaTAP or approved software. Reconcile the withholding liability separately from net wages, federal taxes and unemployment obligations. Keep any final return and account closure evidence with the last payroll.']),
('Vendor Bills And Operating Income',[2],['The dealer statute calls for invoices, shipping papers and other pertinent records supporting taxable purchases and use. Keep a vendor register identifying resale stock, operating supplies, equipment and digital subscriptions. Send invoices with uncertain tax treatment to a purchase review list with their use location.','For the general ledger, identify customer receipts separately from owner advances, borrowings and refunds. Connect supplier credits to the original invoice and customer credits to the original sale. Preserve contracts and financing documents for assets so cost and debt are not confused.']),
('Monthly Statements By Location',[],['A business serving several parishes benefits from a location reconciliation that bridges sales system totals to tax reports and bank settlements. Investigate missing address information before the close. A processor deposit reduced by fees should still connect to the gross sale and tax detail.','Reconcile cash, cards, receivables, bills and debt before delivering financial statements. Keep state and local liability rollforwards beside the balance sheet. Explain payments applied to an earlier period and avoid clearing an unexplained agency balance through miscellaneous expense.']),
('Hypothetical Festival Seller',[1],['Hypothetical example: a Louisiana maker sells at events in two parishes and also ships online orders. The bookkeeper saves each event location, sale detail and shipping address, separates deposits by event, and gives the preparer a jurisdiction summary. The scenario establishes neither an exemption nor a universal filing method for every seller.']),
('Year End And Preservation',[2,4],['Prepare a handoff containing sales by location and product class, exemption evidence, payroll annual reconciliation, inventory counts, asset invoices and unresolved purchase questions. Keep historical tax mappings where a system changed its treatment of digital products.','Louisiana RS 47:309 requires dealer records to remain until the related taxes have prescribed. That is a legal condition requiring review of the relevant periods and any suspension or interruption, rather than an automatic number of years from the invoice. Federal employment tax records have their own minimum four year requirement. Obtain written retention decisions for state payroll, income tax and contested periods before destruction.'])],[('Louisiana State And Parish Tax FAQ','https://revenue.louisiana.gov/tax-education-and-faqs/faqs/fairs-festivals-and-other-special-events/why-do-i-have-to-pay-two-taxes/',['Separate state and parish taxing jurisdictions.']),('Louisiana RS 47:309 Dealer Records','https://legis.la.gov/Legis/Law.aspx?d=101930',['Tangible property, digital products, services, invoice and shipping evidence and preservation until prescription.']),('Louisiana Withholding','https://revenue.louisiana.gov/businesses/widely-used-tax-types/withholding/',['Electronic returns and payment, zero returns, annual reconciliation and account closing.']),('IRS Employment Tax Records','https://www.irs.gov/businesses/small-businesses-self-employed/employment-tax-recordkeeping',['Federal employment tax minimum four year preservation.'])],['Louisiana prescription and current state and local classifications require professional review; no numeric universal state retention period asserted.'])
add('Minnesota','MN','Minnesota bookkeeping for local sales reporting, employee service locations, vendor evidence and unemployment record preservation.','Minnesota employer records include service locations and daily hours, while sales reporting needs state and local tax detail.',[
('Sales And Local Tax Detail',[1],['The Minnesota sales and use tax publication distinguishes registration, local taxes, taxable purchases and reporting. Keep customer delivery evidence, gross sales, refunds and exemption support in a form that can be reconciled to the return. Preserve original and amended filing workpapers together.','For accounts payable, separate purchases consumed by the business from goods intended for resale. Save the vendor invoice and any tax charged elsewhere, and record where the purchase is used. Route uncertain use tax treatment to the preparer before approving the reporting schedule.']),
('Employee Forms And Service Records',[2,3],['Minnesota employers need federal and Minnesota withholding certificates; the new employer publication identifies Form W-4MN. Retain replacements with their effective dates rather than overwriting the earlier employee instructions. Reconcile wages and deductions to the liability accounts and payment confirmations.','The unemployment record rule requires individual service dates, daily hours, location, wages paid and unpaid, pay rate, residence and certain reimbursements. For services across states, it also identifies base of operations, direction and control, and service dates by state. Capture these facts as work occurs so payroll review does not depend on year end recollection.']),
('Income And Vendor Review',[],['A field service company can collect deposits for scheduled work while receiving reimbursements and loan funds in the same bank account. Label each source and maintain the job reference. An unexplained deposit should remain on the owner review list until supported.','Payable records should show which job received materials and whether an item became company equipment. Retain receiving evidence, supplier returns and financing agreements. These practical controls help the preparer distinguish an operating cost from an asset or balance sheet item.']),
('Monthly Reports And Wage Reconciliation',[],['Review revenue and direct spending by completed job, with outstanding deposits and unbilled work explained under the chosen accounting basis. Reconcile the bank, cards, customer ledger and supplier balances before issuing the profit and loss statement and balance sheet.','Compare payroll work location records with the payroll system allocation and investigate differences promptly. Maintain separate schedules for income withholding, unemployment and other employer programs that apply. Return acknowledgments should identify both the reporting period and tax type.']),
('Hypothetical Mobile Repair Crew',[3],['Hypothetical example: a Minnesota repair crew spends part of a week servicing equipment across the state border. The bookkeeper preserves the daily job tickets, hours, location, travel reimbursements and information about where the work is directed. Payroll specialists receive the facts before deciding state allocation; the scenario assumes no reciprocity or automatic exemption.']),
('Year End And Record Preservation',[3],['Provide the preparer with job revenue detail, inventory adjustments, equipment additions, debt balances, wage reconciliations and sales return history. Keep an exception schedule for missing certificates and uncertain purchase taxes, including the person responsible for resolution.','Minnesota Rule 3315.1010 requires the covered employer service records for at least four years in addition to the current calendar year. Apply that specific unemployment record rule without shortening it to four years from an individual paycheck. Sales, income tax, paid leave and property records require their own retention analysis; an amendment deadline is not itself a destruction rule.'])],[('Minnesota Sales And Use Tax Business Publication','https://www.revenue.state.mn.us/book/export/html/10021',['Registration, state and local tax, purchases and amended return support.']),('Minnesota New Employer Publication','https://www.revenue.state.mn.us/new-employer-guide',['Federal and Minnesota withholding certificates.']),('Minnesota Rule 3315.1010','https://www.revisor.mn.gov/rules/3315.1010/',['Employer service record fields, interstate service facts and four years in addition to current calendar year.'])],['Minnesota Paid Leave and other 2026 employer programs require separate review. No statewide sales record destruction date asserted.'])
add('Mississippi','MS','Mississippi bookkeeping for location sales records, tax permits, payroll evidence and the agency retention conflict.','Mississippi records must preserve each business location and the evidence supporting exempt sales; current agency retention statements need careful separation.',[
('Location Records And Exempt Sales',[1],['Mississippi DOR calls for a separate set of sales records for each business location. Exempt sales need documentation, and sales involving permits or material purchase certificates require the relevant transaction and purchaser information. Keep the actual product description when an exemption depends on the item.','As an accounts payable control, save supplier invoices with purchase approvals, payment and the job or location receiving the items. Preserve any permit evidence relied upon and send equipment or supply purchases without tax for review. A permit number alone does not explain how the purchased item was used.']),
('Payroll And Employer Registration',[2],['The agency business FAQ describes withholding registration for covered businesses paying Mississippi residents or nonresidents performing services in Mississippi. Preserve employee identity, home address, employment periods, wage payments and withholding records, then give multistate facts to a payroll professional.','Reconcile employee deductions and employer costs separately. Match the payroll register with payment confirmations and wage statements, and retain amended reports with an explanation. A vendor agreement or information return does not decide whether a worker is an employee.']),
('Income, Inventory And Expense Files',[1],['DOR identifies records supporting income, deductions and credits, including state and federal returns and their schedules. Keep sales, returns and damaged inventory adjustments linked to source documents. A physical stock count should explain quantity differences from the ledger.','For management purposes, classify owner withdrawals and loan repayments separately from operating expenses. Maintain asset invoices with repair and improvement history. Supplier credits and customer bad debts should have a documented reason instead of being cleared through a general adjustment account.']),
('Monthly Reporting Across Locations',[],['Reconcile each location sales report to deposits and interlocation transfers before combining the financial statements. A transfer of inventory should preserve the sending and receiving location so it is not mistaken for an external sale or an unexplained shrinkage adjustment.','Review cash, cards, customer aging and unpaid bills, then present consolidated profit and loss and balance sheet results with location exceptions. Maintain separate schedules for sales tax, purchase tax review and payroll liabilities. Investigate agency notices against the exact tax period rather than against the current month balance.']),
('Hypothetical Two Store Transfer',[1],['Hypothetical example: a Mississippi hardware business moves stock between two stores and later sells some items to a customer claiming an exemption. The bookkeeper preserves the transfer record separately from the customer invoice and links the customer exemption documentation to the actual sale. The example does not determine eligibility for any exemption.']),
('Year End And Conflicting Retention Statements',[1,2],['Give the preparer location sales totals, inventory count adjustments, exemption evidence, asset schedules, payroll support and filed returns. Identify material purchase certificates and unresolved use tax questions separately so they receive substantive review.','The general DOR recordkeeping page states at least three years for business records, while its business FAQ specifies a minimum four year period in the sales record answer and at least three years in its withholding answer. Preserve sales records for at least four years while the discrepancy is resolved with DOR or a qualified adviser; do not present the shorter general statement as a safe sales destruction date. Other holds and record categories can require longer.'])],[('Mississippi Record Keeping And Document Retention','https://www.dor.ms.gov/forms-resources/taxpayer-guidance/record-keeping-document-retention',['Location records, exemption evidence, income and withholding documentation; general three year language.']),('Mississippi Business Tax FAQ','https://www.dor.ms.gov/business/business-tax-frequently-asked-questions',['Withholding registration and records, four year sales retention answer and three year withholding answer.'])],['Official DOR pages conflict on general versus sales retention; conservative four year sales preservation recommended pending qualified resolution. Special industries and workforce retention excluded.'])
add('Missouri','MO','Missouri bookkeeping for sales and use records, active withholding accounts, annual wage evidence and tax audit preservation.','Missouri active withholding accounts require returns even during zero periods, and sales evidence should survive beyond the legal minimum when audit issues remain.',[
('Sales Records And Purchase Use Tax',[1],['Missouri sales instructions identify filed returns, working papers, sales invoices, exemption support, purchase invoices and asset schedules among the records to preserve. Keep the chart of accounts and customer references used to produce the return so totals remain understandable after a software change.','For payables, identify purchases used by the business separately from inventory for resale. Attach the supplier bill, delivery information, tax charged and payment. Uncertain tax treatment belongs on a use tax review schedule with the preparer decision recorded beside the original evidence.']),
('Active Employer Accounts',[2],['Missouri requires an active withholding account to file Form MO-941 even when no tax is due. The current agency explanation also states that Missouri does not honor reciprocity agreements. Keep residence and actual service location evidence for review rather than assuming an employee living across a border qualifies for an exemption.','Use completed Missouri withholding certificates in payroll and preserve changes. Reconcile wage registers, withholding liability, payments and annual statements. When an account closes, save the closure request and final reporting evidence; a quiet payroll period does not explain a missing filing.']),
('Vendor And Income Documentation',[],['A repair business should connect each supplier invoice to parts received, parts returned and the job using them. Separate installed parts from equipment retained by the shop. Retain supplier credit correspondence when a disputed item is settled after the original month closes.','Record customer payments against the invoice ledger and label deposits for future work. Owner contributions, equipment loans and insurance recoveries need their own source evidence. The resulting income statement should not depend on the bank feed guessing what a deposit represents.']),
('Monthly Reporting And Agency Balances',[],['Reconcile operating accounts, credit cards, repair receivables, vendor balances and loans before producing statements. Review uncashed payroll checks and old customer credits with the owner. Explain cash collected for unfinished jobs under the accounting basis agreed with the preparer.','Maintain a liability rollforward for sales and use taxes and for each payroll tax. Compare ledger activity with MyTax Missouri filings and confirmations. A credit appearing on an agency account should be documented and reviewed before it is applied to a future return.']),
('Hypothetical Border Employee',[2],['Hypothetical example: a Missouri repair shop hires an employee who lives in Kansas and performs work at its Missouri premises. The bookkeeper preserves the residence, work location and withholding certificate, sends the facts to payroll review and does not mark the employee exempt solely because the address is outside Missouri. No specific tax amount is assumed.']),
('Year End And Retention',[1,3],['Prepare the year end handoff with sales and purchase tax reconciliations, wage statements, open job deposits, inventory counts, asset and debt schedules and unresolved customer credits. Include amended returns and the reason for every material accounting adjustment.','Missouri identifies at least three years for sales and use tax records under section 144.320. DOR recommends five years or as long as necessary to establish that no liability exists for periods under audit, and extends the same guidance to corporate tax records. Distinguish that recommendation from the minimum; establish separate employer and federal retention schedules before deleting payroll evidence.'])],[('Missouri Sales Tax Detailed Instructions','https://dor.mo.gov/forms/4789.pdf',['Sales, purchase and asset record scope and three year minimum.']),('Missouri Maintain Employer Withholding','https://dor.mo.gov/taxation/business/registration/small-business/maintain/withholding.html',['Active account zero returns, Missouri certificate and no reciprocity agreements.']),('Missouri Tax Audit FAQ','https://dor.mo.gov/faq/taxation/business/tax-audits.html',['Three year statutory sales minimum and DOR five year or audit need recommendation; corporate records guidance.'])],['Local tax sourcing, special district treatment, employer retention and worker classification need individual review.'])
